\title{FedXplain-IoMT: Diagnosing Cross-Protocol Explanation Divergence in Federated Intrusion Detection for Medical IoT}

\author{\uppercase{Swayam Swaroop Sahu}\authorrefmark{1}, 
\uppercase{Prakhar Sharma}\authorrefmark{1}, 
\uppercase{Geraldine Bessie Amali D}\authorrefmark{1}, 
and \uppercase{Kathiravan Srinivasan}\authorrefmark{1}, \IEEEmembership{Senior Member, IEEE}}

\address[1]{School of Computer Science and Engineering, Vellore Institute of Technology, Vellore 632014, India (e-mail: sahuswayamswaroop@gmail.com; prakhar.sharmaa2019@vitalum.ac.in; geraldine.amali@vit.ac.in; kathiravan.srinivasan@vit.ac.in)}

\markboth
{Sahu \headeretal: FedXplain-IoMT: Diagnosing Cross-Protocol Explanation Divergence}
{Sahu \headeretal: FedXplain-IoMT: Diagnosing Cross-Protocol Explanation Divergence}

\corresp{Corresponding author: Geraldine Bessie Amali D, Associate Professor Sr., School of Computer Science and Engineering, Vellore Institute of Technology, Vellore 632014, India (e-mail: geraldine.amali@vit.ac.in).}

\begin{abstract}
A detector can score well across a federation while raising frequent false alarms on one traffic domain. We study this discrepancy in an offline intrusion detection benchmark for the Internet of Medical Things. We partition 166,336 records from the Canadian Institute for Cybersecurity medical-device benchmark into three clients representing Wi-Fi, Message Queuing Telemetry Transport and Bluetooth. Across two random seeds, federated averaging achieved a client-averaged macro F1 of 0.7988, yet recognized only 39.52 percent of benign Bluetooth records. Increasing the proximal penalty reduced mean benign recall across the tested settings. Personalizing the classification head raised mean Bluetooth benign recall to 0.8489, and fixed protocol grouping raised it to 0.9927. On Bluetooth attack examples, the personalized and shared models nevertheless ranked mean absolute Shapley additive explanations almost identically. In a retrospective probe, fitting a slope and intercept to the frozen shared model's Bluetooth logits improved benign recall and probability scores on disjoint evaluation rows at a cost in attack recall. These observations support evaluating the local operating point alongside attribution similarity. They apply to record-level partitions of one benchmark and warrant evaluation on independent captures.
\end{abstract}
\begin{keywords}
Explainable artificial intelligence, federated learning, Internet of Medical Things, intrusion detection, model personalization, probability calibration.
\end{keywords}
\titlepgskip=-15pt
\maketitle
\section{Introduction}\label{sec:introduction}
\subsection{Clinical devices and network security}
An infusion pump delivers a programmed therapy while its network interface may carry configuration updates, status reports or alarms. An electrocardiography monitor samples continuously, a pulse oximeter sends oxygen saturation readings, and a smart bed may report occupancy or movement. The availability, freshness and origin of these messages matter for different reasons. A delayed oxygen alarm can leave a clinician looking at a stale value. An integrity failure can present a plausible but incorrect vital sign. A change to a pump configuration could affect dosage if authorization and device-level safeguards also failed. These are potential consequences. A flow-level attack label cannot establish that any occurred. Threat modeling of portable electrocardiography systems illustrates why the sensing device, gateway and monitoring application warrant separate analysis~\cite{ecg,trustreview,clinicalrisk}.

A network intrusion detection system (IDS) observes communication patterns. Traffic features can reveal unusual timing, excessive connection activity or changes in packet length without exposing the clinical payload. Encryption, shared gateways and intermittent connectivity limit what a detector can infer. Clinical triage therefore also requires device state and authentication logs. Resource-constrained anomaly detectors and specialized medical traffic models address different points in this monitoring pipeline~\cite{edgeids,specialized}.

\subsection{Federated learning and data locality}
\PARstart{A}{n} intrusion detector for the Internet of Medical Things (IoMT) can learn from extracted traffic features pooled across sites. The General Data Protection Regulation and the Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act impose conditions on processing and disclosing identifiable health information~\cite{gdpr,hipaa2013security}. Federated learning (FL) allows sites to retain records while exchanging model parameters~\cite{nguyen2021federated,mcmahan2017communication}. Such updates may still disclose information.

\subsection{Why global scores are insufficient}
Macro F1 gives the benign and attack classes equal weight within a client. Averaging again over clients produces one number for several distinct confusion matrices. A missed attack and a false alarm on a routine observation have different operational consequences. The harmonic means conceal how often either error occurs on a particular domain. We therefore report benign recall (specificity) and attack recall (sensitivity) for each client with the corresponding class supports.

\subsection{Protocol heterogeneity}
Wi-Fi conveys link-layer frames that can carry Internet Protocol traffic. The derived records include transport flags and service indicators. Message Queuing Telemetry Transport (MQTT) describes an application conversation: a client establishes a session with a broker and publishes or subscribes to topics. Many connections and many publications within an established session can impose different costs on the broker. Bluetooth Low Energy exchanges short link-layer payloads and can sleep between connection events. Their timing can reflect a wearable's duty cycle. The host-side Bluetooth captures expose controller packets and connection handles. Native Bluetooth traffic lacks Internet headers. Wi-Fi generally supports higher throughput, while Bluetooth wearables can prioritize energy per report. Specific bandwidth and battery use were not measured.

We simulate three clients drawn from heterogeneous traffic domains. Wi-Fi and Bluetooth Low Energy (BLE) provide link and physical layer technologies. Message Queuing Telemetry Transport (MQTT) provides application-layer messaging over the Transmission Control Protocol. Within Wi-Fi, 96.4\% of records carry an attack label. Wi-Fi also contributes 60.1\% of training records to sample-weighted aggregation. Bluetooth has a benign majority. Our question is how a joint model behaves on each domain when the domains contribute different sample counts and class distributions.

FedXplain-IoMT evaluates established learning methods along three axes: class-specific detection, agreement of input attributions, and transfer to a client lacking particular local attack labels. We distinguish comparing traffic domains under one method from comparing models on the same domain. A separate output-level probe asks whether a fixed model can recover benign recall using only a local adjustment to its scores.

\subsection{Related Work}\label{sec:related_work}
Federated averaging (FedAvg) weights client updates by sample count~\cite{mcmahan2017communication}. Federated proximal optimization (FedProx) penalizes departure from broadcast weights~\cite{li2020federated}. Federated personalization layers (FedPer) retain selected parameters locally~\cite{arivazhagan2019federated}. Sattler and colleagues infer groups from update geometry~\cite{sattler2020clustered}. Our clustered baseline fixes groups before training.

The KDD Cup, UNSW-NB15, Bot-IoT and CICIoT2023 benchmarks cover different network environments~\cite{tavallaee2009detailed,moustafa2015unsw,koroniotis2019towards,neto2023ciciot2023}. Previous work combined federated intrusion detection and Shapley additive explanations (SHAP) on CICIoT2023 distributed across four clients~\cite{fedxai}. Here client membership follows traffic domain, and comparisons use binary-stratified record splits within each domain. We quantify how an explanation changes across domains or across models evaluated on one domain.

Graph-driven personalization learns relationships among clients, semi-decentralized training changes how participants exchange updates, and federated resampling changes the class distribution presented during training~\cite{graph,semidec,smote}. A distributed detector can also evaluate the cost of exchanging updates~\cite{hada}. Chandnani, Amali and colleagues study hyperparameter optimization for federated IoT intrusion detection~\cite{amali}. Here we hold architecture and training schedule fixed while comparing which parameters are shared.

Explainable detectors also use feature information at different stages. One distillation method uses Shapley additive explanations (SHAP) during learning~\cite{distill}, expert fusion organizes features into groups~\cite{experts}, and causal federated models introduce assumptions about relations among variables~\cite{causalfl}. Our post-hoc comparison leaves training unchanged. Its unit of analysis is a pair of model, client and sample configurations.

\subsection{Research gap}
Results from separate benchmarks cannot by themselves identify the effect of a private head. Feature definitions, support for benign traffic, dependence between records and the unit of splitting vary across datasets. IoMT-TrafficData provides a complementary acquisition pipeline~\cite{iottraffic}. Sampling-aware detection and cross-domain transfer studies illustrate how the evaluation population changes the question~\cite{adaptive,transferstudy,drift}. We ask whether local specificity can change substantially when two models give similar aggregate attributions on the same records.

\subsection{Contributions}
\begin{itemize}
\item We compare six training strategies on protocol-grounded clients and document split membership, label counts and exact feature overlap.
\item We apply private heads and fixed protocol groups to the same medical traffic partition. Our contribution is the evaluation of these established strategies against a local-only control under a common measurement protocol.
\item We separate cross-client and within-client explanations and test an output adjustment on frozen checkpoints. Transfer experiments include a source-matched benign control. Adaptation experiments distinguish target-inclusive from target-excluded pretraining.
\end{itemize}
\section{Dataset and Attack Taxonomy}
\subsection{Benchmark and partitions}\label{sec:data}
The Canadian Institute for Cybersecurity Internet of Medical Things 2024 (CICIoMT2024) testbed comprised 25 physical and 15 simulated devices~\cite{dadkhah2024towards}. The subset in Table~\ref{tab:data} draws from labeled Wi-Fi/MQTT feature files and Bluetooth host controller interface (HCI H4) captures. The subset documentation describes a stratified draw with seed 42, nominal caps of 100,000 Wi-Fi and 50,000 MQTT records, and all extracted Bluetooth windows. Filename-derived labels merge numbered variants. The resulting 19 attack labels comprise 13 Wi-Fi, five MQTT and one Bluetooth label, excluding benign. Wi-Fi and MQTT draw benign records from the same source pool.

Bluetooth extraction groups HCI packets by connection handle and one-second window. Size, timing and count statistics describe these windows. Inapplicable Internet Protocol flags and indicators are zero-filled, and the LLC field represents the fraction of asynchronous connection-oriented data packets. Some columns therefore have different meanings across clients. Zero-filled fields can identify the domain. The subset lacks row-to-capture identifiers needed to establish independence or quantify device overlap after sampling.

For each seed we repartition the derived rows 80/20, stratified by binary label within client. Original capture-level test separation is not enforced. Identifier, label and nonnumeric columns are excluded. Nonfinite values are replaced with zero. Each training feature matrix has $n_k$ rows and 45 ordered columns. Its means $\boldsymbol{\mu}_k$ and scales $\boldsymbol{\sigma}_k$ are applied to both splits, with unit scale for constant columns. We checked exact equality of all 45 pre-scaling features at model input precision. At seeds 42 and 7, respectively, 4,329 and 4,417 Wi-Fi test rows matched a training row out of 19,999. Bluetooth had four matches per seed and MQTT had none within client. Feature equality does not identify a shared packet, but this overlap limits interpretation of test performance on novel traffic.

