\subsection{Attack labels and clinical interpretation}
The Canadian Institute for Cybersecurity grouped testbed traffic into denial of service (DoS), distributed denial of service (DDoS), reconnaissance, spoofing and MQTT scenarios. For Wi-Fi, the flood labels distinguish Transmission Control Protocol (TCP), User Datagram Protocol (UDP), Internet Control Message Protocol (ICMP) and TCP synchronization (SYN) traffic. The reconnaissance labels distinguish operating-system identification, host discovery, port enumeration and vulnerability scanning. Address Resolution Protocol (ARP) spoofing concerns the binding between an address and a local link endpoint. The MQTT scenarios distinguish session establishment, publication traffic and malformed messages. The Bluetooth DoS label identifies the source scenario without specifying a common IP-layer mechanism. The released features describe traffic observed during these labeled capture scenarios. Table~\ref{tab:attacks} lists the retained labels. Clinical consequences are possible interpretations for monitoring services.
