\section{System Architecture and Methodology}\label{sec:methods}
\begin{figure*}[t]\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{system_architecture.png}
\caption{Simulated training for three protocol clients. Arrows denote parameter exchange. Records and fitted scalers stay in their partitions during federated training. The pooled comparator is trained separately.}\label{fig:architecture}
\end{figure*}

\subsection{Model and training}\label{sec:training}
For $K=3$ clients let $\mathcal D_k=\{(\mathbf x_k^{(i)},y_k^{(i)})\}_{i=1}^{n_k}$, with $n=\sum_k n_k=133{,}067$, $\mathbf x\in\mathbb R^{45}$ and $y\in\{0,1\}$ (attack is one). The multilayer perceptron uses the following layers:
\begin{align}
\mathbf h_1 &= \operatorname{ReLU}(W_1\mathbf x+\mathbf b_1),\\
\tilde{\mathbf h}_1 &= \operatorname{Dropout}(\mathbf h_1,0.2),\\
\mathbf h_2 &= \operatorname{ReLU}(W_2\tilde{\mathbf h}_1+\mathbf b_2),\\
z &= W_3\mathbf h_2+b_3,\quad \hat p=(1+\exp(-z))^{-1}.
\end{align}
The hidden widths are 64 and 32. ReLU takes the elementwise maximum with zero. Inverted dropout rescales surviving units during training and acts as the identity during evaluation and explanation. The class decision is $\mathbf1\{\hat p\geq0.5\}$. Writing $p_i=\hat p(\mathbf x_k^{(i)};\mathbf w)$ and $y_i=y_k^{(i)}$, the local loss is unweighted binary cross-entropy with natural logarithms:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal L_k(\mathbf w)=-n_k^{-1}\sum_i[y_i\log p_i+(1-y_i)\log(1-p_i)].
\end{equation}
With full participation, the server averages the locally trained vectors $\mathbf w_{k,t+1}$:
\begin{equation}
\mathbf w_{t+1}=\sum_{k=1}^K(n_k/n)\mathbf w_{k,t+1}.
\end{equation}
FedProx takes local optimization steps on
\begin{equation}
F_k(\mathbf w;\mathbf w_t)=\mathcal L_k(\mathbf w)+\tfrac{\mu}{2}\|\mathbf w-\mathbf w_t\|_2^2.
\end{equation}
The penalty includes all trainable weights and biases with the broadcast vector fixed. We test $\mu\in\{0.001,0.01,0.05,0.1\}$.

\subsection{Private heads and protocol groups}
\begin{figure*}[t]\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.9\textwidth]{fig_iomt_mlp_architecture.png}
\caption{Layer widths and the shared and private parameters during FedPer training.}\label{fig:iomt_mlp_architecture}
\end{figure*}
\begin{figure*}[t]\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.9\textwidth]{fig_cluster_fl_topology.png}
\caption{Client training counts and aggregation destinations. The two group servers denote independent logical aggregates.}\label{fig:cluster_fl_topology}
\end{figure*}
FedPer starts all clients with identical seeded heads. Each client updates both $\mathbf w_{\rm trunk}=(W_1,\mathbf b_1,W_2,\mathbf b_2)$ (5,024 parameters) and $\mathbf w_{{\rm head},k}=(W_{3,k},b_{3,k})$ (33 parameters). Only the trunk enters the server average. Each persistent local head is reunited with the aggregated trunk for inference. Fixed Cluster-FL averages all parameters within $\{\mathrm{Wi\!-\!Fi}\}$ and $\{\mathrm{MQTT},\mathrm{Bluetooth}\}$, replacing $n$ with 79,994 and 53,073, respectively. Wi-Fi receives its own update each round.

\subsection{Experimental setup and sensitivity}
Fixing network width and dropout keeps predictive capacity comparable across strategies. The minibatch size and Adam step size control the local update rule. Fixing the number of rounds controls aggregation opportunities, while the epoch budget controls local passes over the data. Sample counts still determine the number of optimizer steps per round. The two seeds vary both split membership and training randomness, so their differences cannot isolate initialization sensitivity.

Only the seed and proximal strength vary in the main runs. The sensitivity report contains paired detection and explanation metrics and the four positive penalties. Rounds, epochs, step size, layer widths, batch size and dropout each have one observed setting. Their marginal effects remain unidentified. The explanation sample and background sizes bound the cost of DeepExplainer and permit matched comparisons across models. Neither size was tuned against test performance.

The simulation uses Flower 1.39.0 client interfaces and PyTorch 2.14.1 on the CPU~\cite{beutel2020flower,paszke2019pytorch}. Training uses Adam at learning rate 0.001, shuffled batches of up to 64 and mean batch loss. Federated clients reset Adam state each round. The final checkpoint follows $T=10$ rounds of $E=3$ epochs. Local-only training uses 30 epochs and pooled centralized training uses ten. With the final partial batch retained, these imply $30\lceil n_k/64\rceil$ steps per client and $10\lceil n/64\rceil$ pooled steps. The main study fixes the budget and threshold without independent validation-based model selection. Raw records stay local during the simulated federated updates. Secure aggregation, differential privacy and defenses against malicious participants lie outside the experiment.

\subsection{Attribution and detection metrics}\label{sec:metrics}
For an analyst investigating an alarm, a score alone gives little indication of whether packet timing, size or protocol indicators influenced the decision. Shapley additive explanations allocate the difference between a prediction and a background reference among input features. DeepExplainer approximates these contributions for differentiable networks. The explanation depends on the chosen background and on whether the explained quantity is a probability or a logit. Taking absolute values removes direction: a large attribution can raise scores on some records and lower them on others.

SHAP 0.53.0 DeepExplainer estimates input contributions to the sigmoid attack probability~\cite{lundberg2017unified}. For each seed and client the sampler resets its seed, draws 100 benign training rows as background and 200 attack test rows for explanation, without replacement. Models run with dropout disabled. Within-client comparisons use the same sampling procedure and feature order. Cross-client comparisons change the test distribution, background and scaler. For feature $j$ on client $k$,
\begin{equation}
\bar\phi_{k,j}=\frac{1}{N_{\rm expl}}\sum_{i=1}^{N_{\rm expl}}|\phi_{k,j}^{(i)}|,\quad N_{\rm expl}=200 .
\end{equation}
For two such vectors $A,B$, let $T_5$ select indices of the five largest components. Equal scores are ordered by original feature index. Reapplying this rule to all saved vectors reproduced every reported top-five list. Higher values of the following metrics indicate greater similarity:
\begin{equation}
J(A,B)=|T_5(A)\cap T_5(B)|/|T_5(A)\cup T_5(B)|.
\end{equation}
With $\mathbf r_A,\mathbf r_B$ the average fractional ranks across the 45 features and $\mathbf u_A=\mathbf r_A-\operatorname{mean}(\mathbf r_A)\mathbf 1$ (likewise for $B$),
\begin{equation}
\rho(A,B)=\frac{\mathbf u_A^\top\mathbf u_B}{\|\mathbf u_A\|_2\|\mathbf u_B\|_2}.
\end{equation}
For nonnegative mean absolute attribution vectors,
\begin{equation}
C(A,B)=\frac{\bar{\boldsymbol\phi}_A^\top\bar{\boldsymbol\phi}_B}{\|\bar{\boldsymbol\phi}_A\|_2\|\bar{\boldsymbol\phi}_B\|_2}.
\end{equation}
$J,C\in[0,1]$ and $\rho\in[-1,1]$ where defined. A constant rank vector makes $\rho$ undefined, and a zero vector makes $C$ undefined. For a specified metric, method and client or pair, the descriptive inter-seed spread is
\begin{equation}
\Delta_{\rm seed}=|M_{42}-M_7|,\qquad S=2.
\end{equation}
For true positives (TP), true negatives (TN), false positives (FP) and false negatives (FN), benign recall is $\mathrm{TN}/(\mathrm{TN}+\mathrm{FP})$, the false-positive rate is its complement, and attack recall is $\mathrm{TP}/(\mathrm{TP}+\mathrm{FN})$. Within-client macro F1 averages the attack and benign F1 scores, $2q/(2q+\mathrm{FP}+\mathrm{FN})$ for $q=\mathrm{TP},\mathrm{TN}$, respectively. A zero denominator contributes zero. The client average weights each of the three macro F1 scores equally. We average before rounding to four decimals. Historical pairwise SHAP metrics are stored to four decimals.

\section{Results}\label{sec:results}
\subsection{Detection by traffic domain}\label{sec:detection}
Tables~\ref{tab:detect42} and~\ref{tab:detect7} and Fig.~\ref{fig:detect} show how the average masks Bluetooth errors. At seeds 42 and 7 the Bluetooth FedAvg confusion counts (TN, FP, FN, TP) were (705,1218,5,1342) and (815,1108,13,1334). Attack recall was 0.9963 and 0.9903, while the false-positive rate was 0.6334 and 0.5762. Local-only had the strongest mean client macro F1 among the decentralized methods. Centralized pooled test macro F1 was 0.9794 and 0.9790. We replayed both centralized checkpoints by client and verified that summing their confusion matrices reproduced the published pooled counts.

