\subsection{FedProx penalty sweep}\label{sec:prox}
Mean Bluetooth benign recall decreased monotonically from 0.1591 to 0.0349, 0.0257 and 0.0208 across the four positive penalties (Fig.~\ref{fig:sweep}). At seed 7 recall rose from 0.0322 to 0.0343 over the final step. These observations are compatible with a cost of constraining local updates on this partition. Gradient conflicts and the effects of aggregation weights require separate measurements.

\begin{figure}[t]\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{fig3_bluetooth_mu_sweep.png}
\caption{Bluetooth benign recall at four categorical penalty settings, with FedAvg at zero for reference. Points are seed means and whiskers give the observed range.}\label{fig:sweep}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Within-client and cross-client explanations}\label{sec:shap}
On Bluetooth, FedPer and FedAvg produced highly correlated rankings of mean absolute SHAP values on attack samples ($\rho=0.9888$ for both seeds). Only three of their top five features overlapped ($J=3/7$). By comparison, applying FedAvg to Wi-Fi and Bluetooth produced $\rho=0.3176$ and $0.2514$, with disjoint top-five sets. Table~\ref{tab:within} compares models within client, while Table~\ref{tab:divergence_results} and Fig.~\ref{fig:heat} compare clients within method. Similar attack attribution ranks can coexist with different benign recall. The results are consistent with a contribution from the classification boundary. Input ranks alone cannot identify latent representation equivalence or probability miscalibration. A companion analysis used the same benign training backgrounds and 200 benign Bluetooth test rows per seed. FedAvg and FedPer had within-client rank correlations of 0.9989 and 0.9978, with top-five Jaccard similarities of 1.0000 and 0.6667 at seeds 42 and 7.

