\subsection{Personalization and fixed groups}\label{sec:recovery}
For seed $s$, let $R_l,R_a,R_p$ denote the Bluetooth benign recalls of local-only, FedAvg and FedPer. Write $\Delta_{\rm gap}=R_l-R_a$ and $\Delta_{\rm rec}=R_p-R_a$. For a positive gap, the recovered percentage is $100\Delta_{\rm rec}/\Delta_{\rm gap}$. Using the displayed recalls,
\begin{align}
\Delta_{\rm gap}^{(42)} &=0.9984-0.3666=0.6318,\\
\Delta_{\rm rec}^{(42)} &=0.8258-0.3666=0.4592,\\
\Delta_{\rm gap}^{(7)} &=0.9990-0.4238=0.5752,\\
\Delta_{\rm rec}^{(7)} &=0.8721-0.4238=0.4483.
\end{align}
FedPer recovered 72.7\% and 77.9\% of the gap. Fixed grouping achieved mean Bluetooth benign recall of 0.9927. The MQTT and Bluetooth clients still shared a model in this configuration. Mean client macro F1 was 0.9241 for FedPer, 0.9665 for Cluster-FL and 0.9708 for local-only. Figure~\ref{fig:trade} compares these detection results with the average of all three cross-client rank correlations.

\begin{figure}[t]\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{fig4_tradeoff_scatter.png}
\caption{Client-averaged macro F1 versus $\bar\rho=2\sum_s\sum_{k<l}\rho_{kl}^{(s)}/[SK(K-1)]$. Horizontal ranges use per-seed pair means and vertical ranges use per-seed client means.}\label{fig:trade}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Retrospective Bluetooth calibration probe}\label{sec:probe}
We froze each FedAvg checkpoint and verified its Bluetooth confusion counts against the main experiment. Within its original 3,270 held-out records, a further binary-stratified split allocated 1,635 rows to fitting and 1,635 to evaluation. A regularized logistic regression ($C=1$) fitted $\sigma(az+b)$ to the scalar pre-sigmoid logit. The decision threshold remained 0.5. Positive temperature scaling $\sigma(z/T_{\rm cal})$ alone preserves the sign of $z$ and hence this decision. The fitted intercept permits a change of operating point.

\begin{table}[t]
\caption{Frozen FedAvg with and without a local slope and intercept on the same evaluation rows. Lower probability scores are better.}\label{tab:probe}
\centering\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{\begin{tabular}{llrrrr}\hline
Seed & Model & Benign recall & Attack recall & Brier & Log loss\\\hline
42 & baseline & 0.3649 & 0.9985 & 0.2913 & 1.2236\\
42 & platt & 0.9158 & 0.9673 & 0.0489 & 0.1783\\
7 & baseline & 0.4204 & 0.9866 & 0.2364 & 1.1005\\
7 & platt & 0.9792 & 0.9659 & 0.0249 & 0.1419\\
\hline\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

Figure~\ref{fig:reliability} compares mean predicted probability with the observed attack fraction in ten equal-frequency bins per model. Both fits reduced Brier score and log loss and recovered benign recall at a cost in attack recall (Table~\ref{tab:probe}). These results show that a scalar adjustment sufficed to change performance on these rows. They leave open why the original federated model produced its scores and whether FedPer changed its hidden features.

