\caption{Reliability on the held-out half of each retrospective Bluetooth split. Points summarize evaluation rows grouped by predicted probability.}\label{fig:reliability}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Cross-protocol attack transfer}\label{sec:transfer}
We apply a target client's model (and its private head for FedPer) to foreign test feature vectors. Each vector uses the target's train-fitted scaler and a decision threshold of 0.5. The Bluetooth training partition contains benign and Bluetooth denial-of-service examples. Its transfer test takes the five MQTT attack labels from MQTT and the 13 Wi-Fi attack labels from Wi-Fi. For the MQTT target, the foreign sources are Wi-Fi and Bluetooth. Client membership thus specifies training visibility for these disjoint attack inventories. This is an offline feature-domain transfer test. It makes no assumption that a Bluetooth link carries MQTT or Address Resolution Protocol attacks.

For each foreign attack label we subtract the flag rate on benign test records from the same source client from the attack recall. We average these margins equally across tested attack labels and seeds. Table~\ref{tab:transfer} shows why a benign control matters. On the Bluetooth target FedPer flagged nearly all foreign benign records. The saved transfer runs use separately generated checkpoints and their in-domain scores can differ from those in the main study.

