\subsection{Adaptation with limited local data}\label{sec:scarcity}
For each target, train from scratch or fit a new 33-parameter head to a frozen trunk. One trunk saw the target's entire training partition during pretraining. A second trunk excluded the target completely. No trunk uses target test labels for fitting. For each of two split seeds and three stratified draws per seed, the adaptation set contains $N=50,100,250,500,1000,5000$ or all target training records (Table~\ref{tab:data}). The scaler is fitted anew on each draw. Each arm uses batches of up to 64 and at least ten epochs, rounded up to whole epochs sufficient for at least 5,000 optimizer steps. All arms are evaluated on the target test partition. The six runs share two test splits.

At $N=50$ on Wi-Fi, mean macro F1 was 0.8136 from scratch, 0.8684 with target-inclusive pretraining and 0.8367 with target-excluded pretraining. The largest difference therefore includes prior access to the target. The serialized budget-wise results contain every draw, optimizer-step count and checkpoint provenance. These results concern adaptation under a specified pretraining regime.
\section{Discussion}\label{sec:discussion}
\subsection{Clinical implications}
Frequent false alarms could impose a review burden or trigger unnecessary containment under a clinical deployment policy. The benchmark measures neither clinical outcomes nor operational costs. Local-only remains a strong comparator on familiar traffic. In the transfer study the relative utility of sharing depends on the target, attack domain and tolerated false-positive rate.

\subsection{Limits of inference}\label{sec:limits}
One benchmark subset, three simulated partitions and two seeds provide descriptive evidence. An absolute seed difference supplies neither an uncertainty threshold nor a significance test. The seeds change both initialization and row splits. Adjacent windows, duplicates or shared benign sources may span partitions. Capture- or device-grouped external tests and repeated independent splits are needed for uncertainty estimates at a meaningful unit. The retrospective calibration evaluation uses a subdivision of an already studied test partition. A prospectively reserved validation set and untouched independent test captures remain necessary.

Mean absolute input attributions discard signs and within-record variation. The benign attribution check covers Bluetooth only. Variation from background sampling and individual false-positive explanations remain unquantified. Further comparisons could test a refitted head and full fine-tuning under equal local data access, measure gradient alignment and assess protocol shortcuts. SCAFFOLD uses first-order control variates to correct client drift~\cite{karimireddy2020scaffold}, and FedNova normalizes heterogeneous local updates~\cite{wang2020tackling}. Deterministic seeds and saved metrics aid inspection, but backend-dependent training and missing capture manifests limit reproducibility.

\subsection{Comparison with prior work}
Federated graph personalization and heterogeneity-aware aggregation address differences among clients by changing their interactions~\cite{graph,semidec}. Fixed groups provide a simpler control: groups are specified before training, and improvements can be compared with training locally on the same domain. Under transfer the evaluated inputs come from another domain. Source-matched benign controls show how a detector that raises many alarms can appear sensitive to foreign attacks. This distinction matters when comparing results from few-shot adaptation~\cite{drift} and multi-protocol transfer~\cite{transferstudy}.

\section{Conclusion}\label{sec:conclusion}
Client-averaged detection concealed frequent false alarms on Bluetooth. Private heads and fixed protocol groups improved benign recall under the tested configuration. High within-client agreement of attack attribution ranks coexisted with different benign operating points. A local logit adjustment recovered much of the lost specificity on held-out rows, with a measurable sensitivity cost. Independent captures and matched controls are needed to establish how these trade-offs carry over to other settings.

\section*{Data and code availability}
The study scripts, dashboard and result schemas are described in the project repository at \url{https://github.com/Swayam-Swaroop-Sahu/FedXplain-IoMT}. The supplement generated by \texttt{scripts/review\_supplement.py} records split membership, reconstructed SHAP draws, label visibility, exact feature matches, confusion counts, transfer results and all 378 adaptation runs. The dataset documentation lists source groups and retained labels. Reproduction from derived records and reproduction from the original captures have different provenance requirements.

